"An ETL pipeline diagram explains the movement and transformation stages that convert source data into a usable destination.\n\nUse when: Review a batch integration or warehouse ingestion design. Explain staging, transformation, rejection, and loading responsibilities.\n\nAvoid when: The principal question is source-field derivation or scheduling alone.\n\nVisual grammar: Stage nodes identify extract, load, transform, and validation behaviors. Stores show source, staging, and destination datasets. Arrows name the data or batch crossing each stage boundary.\n\nBuild: Choose one dataset family and declare ETL or ELT ordering. Identify staging and durable handoff points. Add validation, rejected-record, and replay paths that actually exist. Lay out the main pipeline left to right with exception paths below.\n\nCheck: Data ownership and persistence are clear at each handoff. Incremental keys or batch boundaries are stated. Row counts are labeled observed, expected, or unknown with provenance. Exception connectors avoid stage labels and store titles.\n\nPitfalls: Treating load completion as proof of semantic correctness. Omitting reject paths and making lossy transformations appear complete.\n\nExample: CRM exports land in Raw Storage, are normalized and validated, then load Customer Warehouse with rejected rows isolated.\n\nAlternatives: Data lineage diagram, Change-data-capture diagram."
